import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---
<DocLayout title="Development" description="Build the native app and maintain its documentation website.">
  <h2>Build the macOS app</h2><p>Use macOS with Xcode command line tools and Swift 5.9 or later. The release build produces a universal app for Apple Silicon and Intel.</p><CodeBlock code={'git clone https://github.com/apil-khadka/PrivyLock.git\ncd PrivyLock\n./build.sh release\nopen build/PrivyLock.app'} /><p>Set <code>ARCHS=arm64</code> or <code>ARCHS=x86_64</code> for a single architecture. Local builds are ad-hoc signed unless a signing identity is supplied.</p>
  <h2>Code structure</h2><p>The Swift package separates app enforcement, state, and authentication from its AppKit interface. The core uses bundle identifiers and app URLs to identify targets. Changes to unlocking and enforcement need particular care around asynchronous authentication and process termination.</p>
  <h2>Run checks</h2><CodeBlock code="swift test" /><p>The development branch may include more checks and profiling tools than a published tag. Follow its README for the supported test and profiling commands. Do not run destructive enforcement experiments against apps containing unsaved work.</p>
  <h2>Run the website locally</h2><p>The documentation is a separate Node package in <code>docs/</code>. It uses Astro to generate static pages. Node 22.12 or later is required.</p><CodeBlock code={'cd docs\nnpm ci\nnpm run dev'} /><p>Open the local URL with the <code>/PrivyLock/</code> base path. Shared layouts and components keep navigation, typography, and colors consistent.</p>
  <h2>Update release information</h2><CodeBlock code={'npm run sync:github\nnpm run check'} /><p>The sync command reads public GitHub repository and release metadata. If GitHub is unavailable, a local build can use the committed snapshot. Deployment uses strict syncing so failures do not silently publish stale release metadata.</p><p>GitHub Pages rebuilds on documentation changes, published or edited releases, successful app-release workflows, and a six-hour schedule. The browser independently refreshes the star count, with a five-minute cache. GitHub Actions uses its built-in token only while building; no credentials are shipped to visitors.</p>
  <h2>Contribute</h2><p>Keep installation and security claims tied to released behavior. Add guides under <code>docs/src/pages/docs/</code>, update the documentation navigation, and run <code>npm run check</code> before submitting a change. Release notes come from GitHub releases rather than a second handwritten version list.</p>
</DocLayout>
